1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,780
Imagine someone walks up to you on the street completely serious and just tells you that the invisible bedrock of the universe is a lie.

2
00:00:07,420 --> 00:00:09,940
That is definitely a bold way to start a conversation.

3
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:18,320
Right. And they aren't some conspiracy theorists talking about aliens or fake moon landings. They are a mathematician.

4
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:21,660
Which instantly makes it, you know, a hundred times more intriguing.

5
00:00:21,660 --> 00:00:34,160
Exactly. And they are holding a stack of computer code claiming that all of modern logic, the very math that keeps airplanes in the sky and encrypts your bank account, is fundamentally and ontologically corrupt.

6
00:00:34,580 --> 00:00:36,200
Because of what, exactly?

7
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:46,220
Well, they claim it's corrupt because it believes in a concept called nothing, it embraces arbitrary choices, and it stubbornly ignores the physical reality of a jerk.

8
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:51,340
A jerk. Okay, yeah, you would probably be confused, maybe a little amused by that.

9
00:00:51,660 --> 00:00:56,240
But if you're anything like me, you'd be desperate to know what on earth they are actually talking about.

10
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:59,700
Oh, absolutely. Because it's a phenomenal starting point for a mystery.

11
00:01:00,220 --> 00:01:00,940
It really is.

12
00:01:00,940 --> 00:01:07,720
You are looking at a system, like mathematics and formal logic, that works perfectly well for billions of people every single day.

13
00:01:08,580 --> 00:01:13,700
And yet, this person is telling you that the bedrock underneath it is a metaphysical hallucination.

14
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:20,360
So if you are listening to this and thinking, why do I care about the philosophy of a math software, just stick with us.

15
00:01:20,360 --> 00:01:32,900
Yeah, because unraveling this mystery accidentally explains how a computer thinks, why human language is so messy, and, bizarrely enough, it might even offer a mathematical explanation for gravity.

16
00:01:33,440 --> 00:01:34,300
It's wild.

17
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:43,780
Today, we are exploring a highly technical, absolutely crazy controversy playing out right now in the world of formal logic and computer-assisted proofs.

18
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:49,120
And our guide for this deep dive is a pair of extensive papers by an independent researcher named Flyxion.

19
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:52,060
Right. Published in October 2026.

20
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:58,420
They're called Formal Failure and Ontological Overreach, and Rebuilding the Foundations of Logic.

21
00:01:58,820 --> 00:02:02,360
What makes these papers so vital is that they aren't just dry mathematical theory.

22
00:02:02,760 --> 00:02:03,540
No, not at all.

23
00:02:03,540 --> 00:02:07,960
They represent a forensic breakdown of an actual philosophical rebellion.

24
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:12,240
They look at how we translate human reasoning into machine code.

25
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:17,280
And, maybe more importantly, what it actually means when that translation completely breaks down.

26
00:02:17,460 --> 00:02:17,940
Precisely.

27
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:19,480
So, let's set the stage here.

28
00:02:19,880 --> 00:02:23,500
There is a major theorem-proving software out there called Lean.

29
00:02:23,740 --> 00:02:24,360
Yeah, Lean.

30
00:02:24,360 --> 00:02:30,000
Now, I've heard of it in passing, but for someone who doesn't code math for a living, what exactly is Lean?

31
00:02:30,540 --> 00:02:36,320
Think of Lean as an incredibly strict, like, absolutely uncompromising spell checker.

32
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:39,340
But instead of checking your spelling, it checks your logic.

33
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:40,720
Okay. That makes sense.

34
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:45,120
For decades, mathematicians would write proofs on chalkboards or in journals.

35
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:48,420
Human beings are fallible, right? We skip steps.

36
00:02:48,660 --> 00:02:49,200
Oh, definitely.

37
00:02:49,460 --> 00:02:54,080
We say things like, it is obvious that, when maybe it isn't so obvious.

38
00:02:54,080 --> 00:02:59,540
Exactly. Lean is a software environment where you write your proof in a programming language,

39
00:02:59,720 --> 00:03:05,440
and the computer's kernel verifies every single logical step, down to the foundational axioms.

40
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:08,100
So, it doesn't let you get away with anything?

41
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:09,240
Nothing at all.

42
00:03:09,760 --> 00:03:16,120
If Lean says your proof is correct, you have a mathematical guarantee that there are no skipped steps and no human errors.

43
00:03:16,580 --> 00:03:22,240
It's used to verify everything from pure mathematics to the software that runs self-driving cars.

44
00:03:22,240 --> 00:03:28,320
Okay. So, it is the ultimate objective referee. But apparently, some people think this referee is taking bribes.

45
00:03:28,660 --> 00:03:30,520
Which is a heavy accusation.

46
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:38,480
There is a group circulating documents around a new framework called TONE, and they are releasing manifestos with dramatic titles like

47
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:42,340
The Ontological Assumptions of Lean and The Death of ZFC.

48
00:03:42,340 --> 00:03:45,720
They are literally claiming Lean is dead.

49
00:03:46,020 --> 00:03:50,240
Right. They don't just claim it's dead. They claim it is philosophically toxic.

50
00:03:50,900 --> 00:03:55,840
Yeah. TONE presents a four-step argument to prove that Lean is ontologically false.

51
00:03:56,820 --> 00:04:01,400
And Flagstreet's first paper is basically a meticulous deconstruction of this exact argument.

52
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:05,580
I want to walk through their four steps, but I want to make sure I'm getting the logic right.

53
00:04:06,180 --> 00:04:13,040
So, step one, TONE claims that Lean, the software, is entirely equivalent to something called ZFC.

54
00:04:13,640 --> 00:04:15,240
That's their starting premise, yes.

55
00:04:15,780 --> 00:04:21,820
Then step two, they argue that ZFC is committed to the existence of the empty set and the axiom of choice,

56
00:04:22,260 --> 00:04:24,420
both of which TONE considers to be false concepts.

57
00:04:24,840 --> 00:04:29,700
Right. So, they are assigning a very specific philosophical baggage to the software itself.

58
00:04:29,700 --> 00:04:33,780
Then we get to step three, which takes a weird pivot into physics.

59
00:04:34,340 --> 00:04:37,220
TONE rejects those mathematical concepts and asserts that

60
00:04:37,220 --> 00:04:41,280
jerk is the highest admissible form of change in the physical universe.

61
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:46,180
And jerk is a real term in physics, for the third derivative of position.

62
00:04:46,720 --> 00:04:54,000
Right. So, they sit down at their computer, they try to program this truth about jerk into Lean,

63
00:04:54,220 --> 00:04:57,820
and the software just crashes. The code collapses.

64
00:04:57,820 --> 00:05:01,440
Which brings them to step four, their grand conclusion.

65
00:05:02,260 --> 00:05:05,740
Because the code collapsed when they inputted their specific philosophy,

66
00:05:06,520 --> 00:05:11,800
they declared that Lean, and therefore ZFC, is ontologically corrupt.

67
00:05:12,520 --> 00:05:13,040
Wow.

68
00:05:13,360 --> 00:05:17,380
Yeah. The machine rejected their truth, so the machine must be broken.

69
00:05:18,020 --> 00:05:21,160
I'm really trying to wrap my head around that leap in logic.

70
00:05:21,160 --> 00:05:26,460
It sounds, I don't know, it sounds like someone trying to write a sci-fi novel in Microsoft Word.

71
00:05:27,280 --> 00:05:28,680
Okay. I like where this is going.

72
00:05:29,080 --> 00:05:32,800
They don't know how to use page bricks, the formatting gets completely messed up,

73
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:37,640
the file crashes, and they stand up and declare that the English language itself

74
00:05:37,640 --> 00:05:41,140
is structurally incapable of understanding time travel.

75
00:05:41,140 --> 00:05:46,300
That analogy hits the nail on the head. But it actually goes even deeper mathematically.

76
00:05:46,880 --> 00:05:47,200
Really?

77
00:05:47,480 --> 00:05:52,480
Yeah. What you were describing is a classic category error, which Flyxion points out immediately.

78
00:05:53,460 --> 00:05:58,320
The authors of Tone are treating a formal syntax failure as a metaphysical victory.

79
00:05:58,860 --> 00:05:59,940
Which is a huge jump.

80
00:06:00,200 --> 00:06:06,140
It is. But more fundamentally, they're committing a logical fallacy known as affirming the consequent.

81
00:06:06,460 --> 00:06:08,260
Wait, affirming the consequent?

82
00:06:08,260 --> 00:06:14,480
Let's look at the structure of their claim. They're saying if ZFC were ontologically corrupt,

83
00:06:14,940 --> 00:06:16,600
its formalizations would break.

84
00:06:16,880 --> 00:06:18,020
Okay. Sure.

85
00:06:18,400 --> 00:06:25,360
Our formalization broke. Therefore, ZFC is corrupt. It is identical to saying if it is raining,

86
00:06:25,620 --> 00:06:30,380
the streets are wet. I look outside and the streets are wet. Therefore, it must be raining.

87
00:06:30,380 --> 00:06:34,960
Ah, I see. But maybe a water main broke. Or someone washed their car.

88
00:06:34,960 --> 00:06:39,340
Exactly. The streets being wet doesn't guarantee rain at all.

89
00:06:39,880 --> 00:06:40,500
Right. Right.

90
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:47,760
A formalization in a software like Lean can break for dozens of reasons. You might have a typo. You

91
00:06:47,760 --> 00:06:49,520
might have declared a variable incorrectly.

92
00:06:49,620 --> 00:06:52,520
Or introduced two axioms that just contradict each other.

93
00:06:52,900 --> 00:06:58,580
Precisely. None of those failures have anything to do with the foundational philosophy of mathematics.

94
00:06:58,580 --> 00:07:04,680
Tone took a localized software error and diagnosed it as a philosophical revolution.

95
00:07:05,360 --> 00:07:11,800
Okay. So their conclusion is deeply flawed right out of the gate. But Flyxion doesn't just stop at the logical fallacy, do they?

96
00:07:12,140 --> 00:07:18,800
No. Flyxion actually goes backward and methodically dismantles the very premises of Tone's argument.

97
00:07:18,800 --> 00:07:26,580
Let's start with the very first claim, then, that Lean is equivalent to ZFC. What is ZFC, and why is Tone wrong about this?

98
00:07:26,580 --> 00:07:36,660
So ZFC stands for Zermelo-Frankel set theory with the axiom of choice. For the last century or so, it has basically been the standard foundation of mathematics.

99
00:07:37,100 --> 00:07:37,620
Okay.

100
00:07:37,740 --> 00:07:45,540
If you learned about sets in high school, like Venn diagrams, unions, intersections, you were implicitly learning a simplified version of ZFC.

101
00:07:45,540 --> 00:07:49,960
So it's a universe where absolutely everything is built out of sets?

102
00:07:50,380 --> 00:07:58,660
Exactly. The number zero is the empty set. The number one is a set containing the empty set. It's sets all the way down.

103
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:04,160
So Tone thinks Lean is just a digital version of that specific set theory universe.

104
00:08:04,660 --> 00:08:14,820
They do. And it is just factually incorrect. Lean's underlying architecture, the very kernel of the software, does not use set theory at all.

105
00:08:14,820 --> 00:08:15,580
It doesn't.

106
00:08:15,800 --> 00:08:19,480
No. It implements something called a dependent type theory.

107
00:08:19,740 --> 00:08:28,820
Okay. Stop right there. Dependent type theory. That sounds incredibly intimidating. How is that functionally different from the set theory I learned in school?

108
00:08:29,120 --> 00:08:33,900
It's a great question because the distinction is crucial to understanding why the software works.

109
00:08:33,900 --> 00:08:40,860
In set theory, you have objects, and then you have buckets called sets, and you drop the objects into the buckets.

110
00:08:41,320 --> 00:08:45,720
So the fundamental question is always like, is this object inside this bucket?

111
00:08:46,280 --> 00:08:47,960
Right. That's a membership property.

112
00:08:47,960 --> 00:08:57,580
In type theory, things are fundamentally different. You don't have separate buckets. The type is a tag permanently attached to the object itself.

113
00:08:57,960 --> 00:08:58,920
Oh, like a barcode?

114
00:08:59,360 --> 00:09:04,140
Very much like a barcode. An object in Lean doesn't exist independently of its type.

115
00:09:04,740 --> 00:09:09,820
You don't have a generic number three that you later decide to put into the integer bucket.

116
00:09:09,820 --> 00:09:15,380
So the thing you create is intrinsically a three-of-type integer?

117
00:09:16,180 --> 00:09:25,300
Exactly. And because it's dependent type theory, the types can actually depend on values. You could have a type that is arrays of length n.

118
00:09:25,300 --> 00:09:30,040
So if n is 5, that's a completely different type than if n is 6.

119
00:09:30,620 --> 00:09:31,260
You got it.

120
00:09:31,660 --> 00:09:35,260
So how does ZFC fit into this if Lean doesn't even use buckets?

121
00:09:36,160 --> 00:09:41,320
ZFC is just one mathematical library you can choose to build inside Lean's type theory.

122
00:09:42,460 --> 00:09:47,220
Lean is the operating system. ZFC is just an app you can install.

123
00:09:47,640 --> 00:09:48,540
Oh, wow.

124
00:09:48,760 --> 00:09:54,620
You can also build the rules of chess, or quantum mechanics, or a totally different mathematical foundation.

125
00:09:55,300 --> 00:09:59,600
The direction of dependence is the complete opposite of what Tone claims.

126
00:10:00,200 --> 00:10:05,000
Because Lean doesn't rely on ZFC. ZFC relies on Lean to host it.

127
00:10:05,540 --> 00:10:15,500
Exactly. To say Lean is corrupt because you have issues with ZFC is like saying your computer's motherboard is broken because you don't like the rules of a video game you've downloaded.

128
00:10:16,060 --> 00:10:18,480
That completely invalidates their first step.

129
00:10:18,840 --> 00:10:24,540
That's hilarious. But let's look at their second step, because this gets into the philosophy of nothing.

130
00:10:24,540 --> 00:10:35,040
Right. The empty set. Tone claims that ZFC and Lean name nothing and treat it as a concrete object. They are deeply offended by the empty set.

131
00:10:35,540 --> 00:10:44,900
They find it philosophically abhorrent to treat nothingness as an entity. But Flyxion draws a vital distinction here. An empty set is not nothingness.

132
00:10:44,900 --> 00:10:50,820
I'm trying to picture this. If I have an empty cardboard box on my table, the box isn't nothing.

133
00:10:51,040 --> 00:10:51,820
No, of course not.

134
00:10:52,020 --> 00:10:57,760
It's a box. It's made of cardboard. It just happens to have zero items inside it. The container still physically exists.

135
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:07,980
That is a highly functional way to visualize it. Mathematically, the empty set is a highly specific, useful object. It is the identity element for union.

136
00:11:07,980 --> 00:11:12,280
Meaning if you combine any set with the empty set, you just get the original set back.

137
00:11:12,540 --> 00:11:22,000
Exactly. It is also the intersection of two disjoint sets. If I ask you for the set of all square circles, the answer is the empty set.

138
00:11:22,480 --> 00:11:24,360
Because there are no square circles.

139
00:11:24,620 --> 00:11:34,700
Right. The axioms of set theory do not say the void exists as a metaphysical entity. They just say there is a valid mathematical container that has no members.

140
00:11:34,700 --> 00:11:45,400
But Tone actually points to a specific line of code in Lean to prove their point. The text mentions, they quote this line, def empty exists dot type dot dot equals empty.

141
00:11:45,700 --> 00:11:46,560
I remember that part.

142
00:11:47,100 --> 00:11:51,860
Tone looks at that and says, look right there, the software is explicitly declaring that nothingness exists.

143
00:11:52,020 --> 00:11:57,740
Which demonstrates a profound misunderstanding of how compilers allocate memory and define types.

144
00:11:58,000 --> 00:11:58,440
How so?

145
00:11:58,740 --> 00:12:04,420
In Lean, empty is a classifier. It is a type defined with zero constructors.

146
00:12:04,700 --> 00:12:13,840
When you write def empty exists dot type equals empty, you are not summoning a creature called nothing into the universe.

147
00:12:14,360 --> 00:12:17,440
You are simply introducing a constant name for a label.

148
00:12:17,940 --> 00:12:25,100
Okay, let's test that. Are you saying it's like me creating a new folder on my computer desktop and naming it unicorns?

149
00:12:25,620 --> 00:12:26,000
Yes.

150
00:12:26,440 --> 00:12:32,400
Just because I created the folder, it doesn't mean I am asserting that a unicorn is currently roaming around inside my hard drive.

151
00:12:32,400 --> 00:12:36,900
The folder is there, but the operating system knows there are zero files inside it.

152
00:12:37,520 --> 00:12:41,480
And if you tried to pull a file out of that folder, the system would immediately throw an error.

153
00:12:42,480 --> 00:12:47,760
Lean's type checker will actively prevent you from ever producing an inhabitant of the empty type.

154
00:12:48,280 --> 00:12:51,460
But having the folder is essential for the system to function, right?

155
00:12:52,060 --> 00:12:58,040
Flyseen explores the tangible, devastating cost of doing what Tone wants and rejecting the empty type.

156
00:12:58,040 --> 00:13:02,780
Yeah. What actually happens to math if we throw out the empty box?

157
00:13:03,340 --> 00:13:05,680
Well, you lose the standard way we encode negation.

158
00:13:06,400 --> 00:13:07,820
Wait, really? How does that work?

159
00:13:08,300 --> 00:13:11,660
Independent type theory. How do you mathematically say not P?

160
00:13:11,900 --> 00:13:17,640
You don't just put a minus sign in front of it. You define not P as a function. P implies false.

161
00:13:17,640 --> 00:13:26,120
Oh, I see. It means if you were to assume P is true, it would lead you directly to the empty type, which is an absurdity.

162
00:13:26,280 --> 00:13:32,640
Exactly. If you throw out the empty type, you literally lose the ability to say, this equation has no solution.

163
00:13:33,020 --> 00:13:36,380
You essentially crippled the language's ability to disagree.

164
00:13:36,780 --> 00:13:42,280
You do. Every operation that might result in an empty set suddenly becomes a partial operation.

165
00:13:42,280 --> 00:13:49,840
Imagine trying to program the intersection of two shapes on a screen. If they don't touch, the program can't just return an empty set.

166
00:13:50,280 --> 00:13:51,180
What does it do instead?

167
00:13:51,540 --> 00:14:01,620
It has to return something called an option, meaning it might just crash. Or it has to return an error state that you now have to manually manage everywhere in your code.

168
00:14:01,840 --> 00:14:08,900
That sounds like a nightmare. You have to re-describe your entire mathematical universe with massive, clunky workarounds.

169
00:14:08,900 --> 00:14:14,980
So the cost of this pure ontology is that the math becomes incredibly inefficient and buggy.

170
00:14:15,440 --> 00:14:20,160
Wow. Now there's a second thing Tone hates in step two, and that's the axiom of choice.

171
00:14:20,760 --> 00:14:28,780
They claim choice allows for arbitrary distinctions, which they argue turns arbitrary separations into mathematical law.

172
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:32,120
This is another massive equivocation on their part.

173
00:14:32,900 --> 00:14:36,740
Tone is confusing two entirely different mathematical axioms.

174
00:14:36,740 --> 00:14:41,440
They are confusing the axiom of choice with the axiom of separation.

175
00:14:42,140 --> 00:14:44,800
Walk me through the difference. What is the axiom of separation?

176
00:14:45,740 --> 00:14:50,740
Separation is what lets you carve out a subset based on a specific, definable property.

177
00:14:51,320 --> 00:14:58,200
If you have a set of all integers, separation allows you to form a subset of all even integers.

178
00:14:58,560 --> 00:15:02,440
So you apply a concrete, logical formula to filter the original set.

179
00:15:02,440 --> 00:15:08,580
Exactly. That requires a specific rule. It is the exact opposite of arbitrary.

180
00:15:09,600 --> 00:15:14,900
Okay, so separation is rule-based filtering. What is the axiom of choice then?

181
00:15:14,900 --> 00:15:19,740
The axiom of choice addresses a much weirder, infinite scenario.

182
00:15:20,440 --> 00:15:25,960
It says that if you have a collection of non-empty sets, even an infinite number of them,

183
00:15:26,460 --> 00:15:30,940
you can form a new set by picking exactly one element from each of them,

184
00:15:31,420 --> 00:15:34,620
even if you don't have a specific rule for how to pick them.

185
00:15:34,620 --> 00:15:38,700
I can see why that feels a bit slippery. You're just blindly grabbing things from

186
00:15:38,700 --> 00:15:40,400
infinite buckets without a recipe.

187
00:15:41,440 --> 00:15:46,520
It is slippery. The axiom of choice is famous for producing deeply counterintuitive results.

188
00:15:46,940 --> 00:15:49,380
It's the engine behind the Van Ochtarski paradox.

189
00:15:49,660 --> 00:15:50,660
Remind me what that is.

190
00:15:51,400 --> 00:15:54,380
It mathematically proves you can take a solid sphere,

191
00:15:55,060 --> 00:15:57,240
cut it into a few weird scattered pieces,

192
00:15:57,660 --> 00:16:01,440
and reassemble them into two solid spheres of the exact same size.

193
00:16:01,440 --> 00:16:03,920
Which physically makes zero sense.

194
00:16:04,460 --> 00:16:08,820
Which is exactly why many mathematicians over the last century were deeply skeptical of it.

195
00:16:09,280 --> 00:16:12,360
But here is the critical flaw in Tone's argument.

196
00:16:13,140 --> 00:16:17,200
They claim Lean implicitly inherits this controversial axiom of choice

197
00:16:17,200 --> 00:16:20,840
and forces it on you as a secret philosophical bias.

198
00:16:21,280 --> 00:16:22,740
And is it a secret bias?

199
00:16:23,360 --> 00:16:28,040
Not at all. The axiom of choice is famously independent of core ZF-set theory.

200
00:16:28,040 --> 00:16:31,940
You can do mathematics with it, or you can do mathematics without it.

201
00:16:32,400 --> 00:16:34,100
And in Lean, it is never hidden.

202
00:16:34,600 --> 00:16:39,600
It is an explicit, highly visible, auditable axiom called classical dot choice.

203
00:16:40,320 --> 00:16:43,740
This was the part of Flay Axiom's paper that I found incredibly satisfying.

204
00:16:43,800 --> 00:16:48,020
Because if you are a scientist or a philosopher trying to verify a truth,

205
00:16:48,500 --> 00:16:50,840
you want a system that shows you its receipts.

206
00:16:51,360 --> 00:16:51,980
Oh, absolutely.

207
00:16:52,420 --> 00:16:55,600
You want to know exactly what assumptions are holding up your building.

208
00:16:55,720 --> 00:16:57,360
And Lean actually does this.

209
00:16:57,360 --> 00:17:00,080
It does it brilliantly through a footprint system.

210
00:17:00,840 --> 00:17:02,420
Lean doesn't assume you want to use choice.

211
00:17:02,860 --> 00:17:06,400
If you write a simple proof, like proving that 2 plus 2 equals 4,

212
00:17:06,880 --> 00:17:11,100
you can literally type the command hashtag print axioms next to your proof.

213
00:17:11,400 --> 00:17:12,040
And what happens?

214
00:17:12,260 --> 00:17:16,800
The software will output a list of every single foundational axiom your proof relied on.

215
00:17:17,220 --> 00:17:19,700
For basic arithmetic, it will tell you the footprint is empty.

216
00:17:20,240 --> 00:17:23,020
You relied on zero axioms. It was purely computational.

217
00:17:23,420 --> 00:17:24,800
But if you use choice?

218
00:17:24,800 --> 00:17:31,340
If your proof uses the axiom of choice, the software immediately forces you to flag your theorem as non-computable.

219
00:17:31,900 --> 00:17:32,940
It tags the file.

220
00:17:33,460 --> 00:17:38,260
And when you type hashtag print axioms, classical dot choice will flash right there on the screen.

221
00:17:38,540 --> 00:17:39,040
That's amazing.

222
00:17:39,520 --> 00:17:42,100
The software acts like a meticulous accountant.

223
00:17:42,520 --> 00:17:44,280
It stands there with a clipboard and says,

224
00:17:44,440 --> 00:17:48,380
just so you know, you invoked a non-computable arbitrary selection here.

225
00:17:48,380 --> 00:17:53,820
So Lean isn't a corrupt metaphysical overlord forcing you to believe in arbitrary choice.

226
00:17:54,020 --> 00:17:58,660
It's just a tool that gives you the option and then explicitly tracks when you use it.

227
00:17:58,920 --> 00:17:59,220
Exactly.

228
00:17:59,740 --> 00:18:04,220
If Tone wanted to build a constructive mathematical universe without the axiom of choice,

229
00:18:04,820 --> 00:18:06,800
Lean provides the perfect sandbox to do so.

230
00:18:07,180 --> 00:18:11,400
The only consequence is that they wouldn't be able to use some of the pre-built classical math libraries.

231
00:18:11,400 --> 00:18:16,540
But that is a practical engineering cost, not a philosophical impossibility.

232
00:18:17,180 --> 00:18:18,680
Okay, so we've defended the math.

233
00:18:18,980 --> 00:18:23,240
We've shown how Tone misunderstood type theory, the empty set, and the axiom of choice.

234
00:18:23,360 --> 00:18:25,300
But now we really have to talk about the physics.

235
00:18:25,520 --> 00:18:26,660
Oh boy, the physics.

236
00:18:26,880 --> 00:18:30,620
Because this is where Tone's argument goes from a misunderstanding of a software compiler

237
00:18:30,620 --> 00:18:33,520
to a truly bizarre theory of the physical universe.

238
00:18:33,520 --> 00:18:36,380
This brings us to the core of their third premise.

239
00:18:37,040 --> 00:18:41,420
Tone claims that jerk is the highest admissible form of change in the universe,

240
00:18:41,740 --> 00:18:45,060
and anything beyond it is an illusion or a regress.

241
00:18:45,320 --> 00:18:49,880
Let's do a quick physics refresher just so we are all visualizing the same thing.

242
00:18:50,240 --> 00:18:51,700
Position is where you are.

243
00:18:51,920 --> 00:18:52,160
Right.

244
00:18:52,600 --> 00:18:56,080
The first derivative of position is velocity, so how fast you are moving.

245
00:18:56,480 --> 00:19:00,200
The second derivative is acceleration, how fast your velocity is changing.

246
00:19:00,540 --> 00:19:02,340
And the third derivative is jerk.

247
00:19:02,340 --> 00:19:04,640
That's how fast your acceleration is changing.

248
00:19:05,200 --> 00:19:08,240
A good visceral way to feel jerk is being in a car.

249
00:19:08,660 --> 00:19:12,500
If you're going 60 miles an hour constantly, that's velocity.

250
00:19:12,740 --> 00:19:13,440
You feel nothing.

251
00:19:13,840 --> 00:19:13,940
Yeah.

252
00:19:14,200 --> 00:19:17,820
If you hit the gas and speed up, you feel push back into your seat.

253
00:19:18,060 --> 00:19:18,800
That's acceleration.

254
00:19:19,240 --> 00:19:23,780
But the moment you suddenly slam on the brakes, you feel a violent snap forward.

255
00:19:24,060 --> 00:19:26,540
That change in acceleration is jerk.

256
00:19:26,940 --> 00:19:27,180
Right.

257
00:19:27,180 --> 00:19:33,300
And Tone argues that any derivative higher than jerk leads to, quote, regress or incoherence.

258
00:19:33,500 --> 00:19:39,100
They claim that because lean software is silent on the supremacy of jerk, lean is biased.

259
00:19:39,100 --> 00:19:44,000
To which Flyxion responds with a very polite version of, lean is a calculator.

260
00:19:44,860 --> 00:19:46,780
Lean has no built-in physics engine.

261
00:19:47,380 --> 00:19:51,500
It doesn't have native variables for mass or charge or time or gravity.

262
00:19:52,300 --> 00:19:56,680
It's silent on the third derivative for the exact same reason it's silent on the recipe for a chocolate cake.

263
00:19:57,040 --> 00:19:59,060
It only knows what you program into it.

264
00:19:59,480 --> 00:20:01,720
But let's look at their actual physical claim.

265
00:20:02,380 --> 00:20:05,140
Is jerk the highest admissible form of change?

266
00:20:05,140 --> 00:20:07,960
Is anything higher actually incoherent?

267
00:20:08,680 --> 00:20:11,200
It is empirically demonstrably false, right?

268
00:20:11,480 --> 00:20:15,020
Engineers use the fourth derivative of position constantly.

269
00:20:15,540 --> 00:20:17,500
It's casually referred to as snap.

270
00:20:18,240 --> 00:20:19,700
Wait, I saw this in the paper.

271
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:21,960
They use it for drone stabilization, right?

272
00:20:22,280 --> 00:20:22,860
Quad rotors?

273
00:20:23,460 --> 00:20:23,780
Yes.

274
00:20:24,320 --> 00:20:29,800
If you want a quad rotor drone to fly smoothly from point A to point B without jittering or flipping over,

275
00:20:30,180 --> 00:20:32,900
you don't just optimize its velocity or acceleration.

276
00:20:33,640 --> 00:20:34,180
What do you do?

277
00:20:34,180 --> 00:20:39,060
The control algorithms actually generate what are called minimum snap trajectories.

278
00:20:39,480 --> 00:20:44,800
They calculate and minimize the fourth derivative to ensure the motor outputs transition smoothly.

279
00:20:45,300 --> 00:20:48,440
It is a routine, deeply coherent engineering practice.

280
00:20:49,300 --> 00:20:54,240
So Tone's philosophical limit is just factually wrong in modern engineering.

281
00:20:54,800 --> 00:20:55,140
Completely.

282
00:20:55,500 --> 00:20:57,560
But tau doesn't just make a verbal claim.

283
00:20:57,680 --> 00:20:58,860
They try to prove it mathematically.

284
00:20:58,860 --> 00:21:02,920
They propose an equation that they believe should be an axiom of the universe.

285
00:21:03,480 --> 00:21:04,820
J equals 1 over J.

286
00:21:05,100 --> 00:21:09,640
They claim this truncates jerk so that it can only ever equal 1 or negative 1.

287
00:21:09,640 --> 00:21:13,560
And Flyxion absolutely tears this equation to shreds.

288
00:21:13,940 --> 00:21:17,380
It is a bloodbath of logical and physical inconsistencies.

289
00:21:17,820 --> 00:21:18,860
Walk me through it.

290
00:21:19,520 --> 00:21:21,100
Let's look at the algebra first.

291
00:21:21,100 --> 00:21:27,660
If you say J equals 1 over J, and you multiply both sides by J, you get J squared equals 1.

292
00:21:27,980 --> 00:21:30,620
The square root of 1 is 1 or negative 1.

293
00:21:31,080 --> 00:21:35,900
So algebraically, if you treat J as a pure dimensionless number, sure.

294
00:21:36,440 --> 00:21:37,300
But physically?

295
00:21:37,300 --> 00:21:40,740
It fails on three catastrophic levels.

296
00:21:41,880 --> 00:21:42,540
What's the first flaw?

297
00:21:43,320 --> 00:21:44,180
Division by zero.

298
00:21:44,980 --> 00:21:51,040
If your universal law is J equals 1 over J, what happens when jerk is exactly zero?

299
00:21:51,940 --> 00:21:53,400
Well, you can't divide by zero.

300
00:21:54,140 --> 00:21:55,060
The equation breaks.

301
00:21:56,100 --> 00:21:59,680
So you have to outlaw zero jerk from your universe entirely.

302
00:22:00,160 --> 00:22:00,640
Precisely.

303
00:22:01,360 --> 00:22:03,260
And what does zero jerk physically represent?

304
00:22:03,260 --> 00:22:07,300
It represents any object experiencing constant acceleration.

305
00:22:08,200 --> 00:22:08,960
Like dropping an apple.

306
00:22:09,280 --> 00:22:09,480
Yes.

307
00:22:09,980 --> 00:22:13,440
If you drop an apple, it falls under the constant acceleration of gravity.

308
00:22:14,080 --> 00:22:15,040
Its jerk is zero.

309
00:22:15,820 --> 00:22:21,200
If a hockey puck slides across frictionless ice at a constant speed, its acceleration is zero.

310
00:22:21,640 --> 00:22:22,620
And its jerk is zero.

311
00:22:22,960 --> 00:22:30,300
So by trying to force J to be 1 or negative 1, tone has accidentally outlawed gravity, momentum, and inertia.

312
00:22:30,300 --> 00:22:36,020
They wrote a law of physics that makes an apple falling from a tree mathematically impossible.

313
00:22:36,440 --> 00:22:37,000
That is amazing.

314
00:22:37,400 --> 00:22:37,960
It gets worse.

315
00:22:38,620 --> 00:22:40,980
The second flaw is dimensional analysis.

316
00:22:41,680 --> 00:22:43,180
Physical quantities have units.

317
00:22:43,800 --> 00:22:45,900
Jerk is measured in meters per second cubed.

318
00:22:46,360 --> 00:22:50,860
If you take its reciprocal, 1 slash J, the units invert.

319
00:22:51,360 --> 00:22:53,620
It becomes seconds cubed per meter.

320
00:22:53,620 --> 00:22:54,840
Oh, wow.

321
00:22:55,200 --> 00:23:00,300
You cannot set a value of meters per second cubed equal to seconds cubed per meter.

322
00:23:00,840 --> 00:23:03,760
It's like saying five gallons equals one-fifth of a mile.

323
00:23:04,160 --> 00:23:05,600
It is physically meaningless.

324
00:23:06,020 --> 00:23:07,960
I didn't even think about the units.

325
00:23:08,220 --> 00:23:12,140
It's just a raw algebraic manipulation pretending to be physics.

326
00:23:12,340 --> 00:23:17,500
But the third flaw Flyxion points out is about continuity, and this one blew my mind.

327
00:23:17,720 --> 00:23:19,940
The continuity flaw is fatal.

328
00:23:20,460 --> 00:23:21,900
Physical motion is continuous.

329
00:23:21,900 --> 00:23:27,480
An object doesn't instantly teleport from one state to another without passing through the states in between.

330
00:23:27,900 --> 00:23:28,020
Right.

331
00:23:28,540 --> 00:23:37,720
If jerk can only ever equal 1 or negative 1, and it is a continuous function, well, it can never jump across 0 to get from 1 to negative 1.

332
00:23:37,920 --> 00:23:41,860
Because it would have to be 0 at some point, and we already established 0 is outlawed.

333
00:23:42,020 --> 00:23:42,300
Right.

334
00:23:42,600 --> 00:23:44,360
So the jerk must be constant.

335
00:23:44,860 --> 00:23:49,120
It is either always 1 forever or always minus 1 forever.

336
00:23:49,120 --> 00:23:57,320
If the third derivative is a constant, and you integrate it backward to find the position, you get a cubic polynomial, a simple curve.

337
00:23:57,860 --> 00:23:59,320
So what does that rule out?

338
00:23:59,620 --> 00:24:02,640
It rules out almost all complex periodic motion.

339
00:24:02,920 --> 00:24:04,980
It completely rules out the sine function.

340
00:24:05,400 --> 00:24:06,020
Like a pendulum.

341
00:24:06,020 --> 00:24:07,020
Exactly.

342
00:24:07,020 --> 00:24:12,560
If you have a pendulum swinging back and forth, its position is described by a sine wave.

343
00:24:13,180 --> 00:24:14,700
The derivative of sine is cosine.

344
00:24:15,160 --> 00:24:17,560
The derivative of cosine is negative sine.

345
00:24:18,060 --> 00:24:19,040
It loops infinitely.

346
00:24:19,680 --> 00:24:22,980
The jerk of a pendulum is never a constant 1 or negative 1.

347
00:24:22,980 --> 00:24:27,920
So Tone's equation mathematically outlaws a swinging pendulum.

348
00:24:28,560 --> 00:24:28,940
Yes.

349
00:24:29,440 --> 00:24:37,300
So just to recap, Tone inputted an equation that ignores physical units, outlaws gravity, and makes pendulums impossible.

350
00:24:37,600 --> 00:24:40,000
And then they blamed the software when it crashed.

351
00:24:40,640 --> 00:24:40,840
Yep.

352
00:24:40,960 --> 00:24:41,840
That about sums it up.

353
00:24:42,420 --> 00:24:44,800
But Flyxion doesn't just debunk the math.

354
00:24:45,420 --> 00:24:51,140
Flyxion writes a devastating parody to highlight exactly why the logic is fundamentally absurd.

355
00:24:51,140 --> 00:24:58,800
Oh, this is Appendix A, TFUU, the Theory of Immediately Falsified Universals, or as Flyxion jokingly calls it,

356
00:24:59,140 --> 00:25:00,860
Today I've Formalized Unwisely.

357
00:25:01,540 --> 00:25:03,940
It's a brilliant piece of mathematical satire.

358
00:25:04,620 --> 00:25:13,880
Flyxion uses Tone's exact logical structure to prove that the sixth derivative of position is actually the true foundational primitive of the universe.

359
00:25:14,340 --> 00:25:17,100
Because physicists have joke names for the higher derivatives, right?

360
00:25:17,160 --> 00:25:20,680
It goes to velocity, acceleration, jerk, snap, crackle, and pop.

361
00:25:20,680 --> 00:25:22,360
Pop is the sixth derivative.

362
00:25:22,800 --> 00:25:23,100
Exactly.

363
00:25:23,760 --> 00:25:28,280
Flyxion shows that you can write the exact same self-inverting equation for the sixth derivative.

364
00:25:28,920 --> 00:25:29,900
Let P be pop.

365
00:25:30,320 --> 00:25:33,080
Assume the universal law is P equals 1P.

366
00:25:33,800 --> 00:25:37,420
Therefore, by Tone's own logic, P must be 1 or minus 1.

367
00:25:37,800 --> 00:25:39,900
And pop is the ultimate limit of change.

368
00:25:39,900 --> 00:25:42,100
And here is the kicker.

369
00:25:42,560 --> 00:25:50,980
Flyxion actually ran this parody theory through the Lean compiler, and the software checked it and said, yes, this logic compiles perfectly.

370
00:25:51,300 --> 00:25:53,300
Which proves two profound points.

371
00:25:53,780 --> 00:25:56,960
First, the regress argument is perfectly symmetric.

372
00:25:57,460 --> 00:26:02,320
If you demand a metaphysical limit to how many times you can take a derivative, why stop at 3?

373
00:26:02,540 --> 00:26:02,860
Right.

374
00:26:02,860 --> 00:26:08,900
The mathematical definition of a limit is identical whether you are calculating velocity or calculating snap.

375
00:26:09,480 --> 00:26:14,980
You can run Tone's exact algebraic trick at order 3, order 6, or order 100.

376
00:26:15,380 --> 00:26:16,500
And the second point?

377
00:26:16,500 --> 00:26:23,100
The fact that the software compiled the Tifu parody proves that compilation does not equal physical truth.

378
00:26:23,660 --> 00:26:25,740
The machine doesn't know what pop is.

379
00:26:26,160 --> 00:26:28,340
It just checks that the algebraic steps are valid.

380
00:26:29,040 --> 00:26:34,860
Okay, so if Lean is just a calculator, and it isn't ontologically corrupt, we still have a mystery.

381
00:26:35,480 --> 00:26:37,600
Why did Tone's code crash in the first place?

382
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:40,400
They tried to put their jerk theory in, and the system broke.

383
00:26:40,740 --> 00:26:41,940
What actually happened?

384
00:26:41,940 --> 00:26:47,720
This is where Flyxion provides an invaluable taxonomy of how humans interact with machines.

385
00:26:48,340 --> 00:26:55,020
When someone casually says, the software crashed, that phrase obscures a massive spectrum of failure.

386
00:26:55,820 --> 00:26:59,420
Flyxion maps out six distinct layers of failure in a proof assistant.

387
00:27:00,180 --> 00:27:05,340
I want to walk through these layers slowly because this really helped me understand how a computer actually thinks about logic.

388
00:27:05,800 --> 00:27:06,900
What is layer 1?

389
00:27:07,420 --> 00:27:08,520
Layer 1 is parsing.

390
00:27:08,900 --> 00:27:10,660
This is the most mundane failure.

391
00:27:10,660 --> 00:27:12,240
It is a syntax error.

392
00:27:12,900 --> 00:27:13,680
You missed a comma.

393
00:27:14,100 --> 00:27:15,400
You forgot a parenthesis.

394
00:27:15,980 --> 00:27:18,920
Or you used a variable name you haven't defined yet.

395
00:27:19,700 --> 00:27:23,860
The machine looks at your text and essentially says, I don't even recognize this as a sentence.

396
00:27:24,180 --> 00:27:24,580
Exactly.

397
00:27:25,180 --> 00:27:26,960
It has zero philosophical weight.

398
00:27:27,520 --> 00:27:28,600
Okay, simple typo.

399
00:27:29,240 --> 00:27:31,040
Layer 2 is elaboration.

400
00:27:31,120 --> 00:27:31,760
How does that work?

401
00:27:32,380 --> 00:27:37,900
Elaboration is where the system successfully reads your text, but realizes the types are clashing.

402
00:27:38,340 --> 00:27:40,600
The paper gives a fantastic example.

403
00:27:41,240 --> 00:27:43,920
Expecting a proposition, but giving it a boolean.

404
00:27:44,400 --> 00:27:49,100
Wait, a proposition and a boolean, aren't they both just true or false?

405
00:27:49,820 --> 00:27:51,460
In human conversation, yes.

406
00:27:51,800 --> 00:27:54,800
In type theory, they are fundamentally different beasts.

407
00:27:55,620 --> 00:27:57,500
A boolean is a simple data type.

408
00:27:57,720 --> 00:28:00,240
It's a bit of memory flipped to one or zero.

409
00:28:00,240 --> 00:28:06,080
A proposition is a logical statement that requires a mathematical proof to inhabit it.

410
00:28:06,420 --> 00:28:06,780
Oh, I see.

411
00:28:07,180 --> 00:28:12,440
If you write a function that expects a complex logical proposition, and you accidentally feed

412
00:28:12,440 --> 00:28:15,760
it a simple boolean zero, the elaboration engine halts.

413
00:28:16,300 --> 00:28:21,960
Not because ZFC is dead, but because you tried to plug a USB cable into an HDMI port.

414
00:28:21,960 --> 00:28:24,420
It's a localized structural mismatch.

415
00:28:24,800 --> 00:28:26,460
That makes perfect sense.

416
00:28:27,120 --> 00:28:28,400
So what is Layer 3?

417
00:28:29,520 --> 00:28:31,660
Layer 3 is tactic failure.

418
00:28:32,340 --> 00:28:37,320
When you write a proof in Lean, you don't always write every single micro step yourself.

419
00:28:37,660 --> 00:28:38,780
You use tactics.

420
00:28:38,780 --> 00:28:44,000
These are automated search algorithms that explore a decision tree to find the missing

421
00:28:44,000 --> 00:28:44,820
steps for you.

422
00:28:45,100 --> 00:28:46,760
Like a GPS recalculating.

423
00:28:46,960 --> 00:28:47,260
Yeah.

424
00:28:47,800 --> 00:28:49,100
Sometimes a tactic fails.

425
00:28:49,480 --> 00:28:52,720
It searches for 10 seconds, gets lost in a loop, and gives up.

426
00:28:53,520 --> 00:28:57,840
The theorem might be perfectly true, but the automated pathfinder just wasn't smart enough

427
00:28:57,840 --> 00:28:58,860
to reach the destination.

428
00:28:59,580 --> 00:29:02,720
So a tactic failure is just the GPS recalculating forever.

429
00:29:03,460 --> 00:29:06,280
Then we get to Layer 4, which is a kernel failure.

430
00:29:06,960 --> 00:29:08,600
This one is exceptionally rare.

431
00:29:09,280 --> 00:29:13,480
The kernel is the tiny, independently audited core of the software that does the final

432
00:29:13,480 --> 00:29:14,280
check on everything.

433
00:29:14,800 --> 00:29:19,900
If a fully elaborated proof fails at the kernel, it usually means you've discovered a genuine

434
00:29:19,900 --> 00:29:21,140
bug in the software itself.

435
00:29:21,540 --> 00:29:25,940
Like the software either accepted a fake proof or rejected a valid one.

436
00:29:26,340 --> 00:29:26,640
Right.

437
00:29:27,320 --> 00:29:31,840
But again, this is a software engineering defect, not a metaphysical crisis.

438
00:29:32,060 --> 00:29:35,920
Which brings us to Layer 5, derived contradiction.

439
00:29:35,920 --> 00:29:40,960
Flyoxion says this is the only failure that actually matters fundamentally.

440
00:29:41,800 --> 00:29:44,360
It is the only one with philosophical teeth.

441
00:29:44,960 --> 00:29:50,040
A derived contradiction means the system perfectly understood your text, the types matched, the

442
00:29:50,040 --> 00:29:55,060
tactics worked, the kernel checked it, and it successfully proved that your assumptions

443
00:29:55,060 --> 00:29:59,120
lead directly to a logical contradiction, like 0 equals 1.

444
00:29:59,120 --> 00:30:02,740
So it proved that your ideas are mathematically suicidal.

445
00:30:02,740 --> 00:30:03,220
Exactly.

446
00:30:03,720 --> 00:30:08,440
It means the new axioms you fed into the system fundamentally clash with the host system's

447
00:30:08,440 --> 00:30:09,060
native logic.

448
00:30:09,820 --> 00:30:12,040
And finally, Layer 6 is scope.

449
00:30:12,040 --> 00:30:16,740
Scope failure is insidious, because the software doesn't crash at all.

450
00:30:17,380 --> 00:30:19,620
Everything compiles perfectly, no contradictions.

451
00:30:20,220 --> 00:30:24,240
But the theorem you proved doesn't actually map to the real world in the way you think

452
00:30:24,240 --> 00:30:24,640
it does.

453
00:30:25,080 --> 00:30:26,600
Just like the parody of POP.

454
00:30:26,960 --> 00:30:30,280
It compiled, but its scope regarding physical reality was zero.

455
00:30:30,620 --> 00:30:31,080
Exactly.

456
00:30:31,740 --> 00:30:37,240
So looking at those six layers, Tone never actually tells us which layer their code failed

457
00:30:37,240 --> 00:30:37,480
at.

458
00:30:37,800 --> 00:30:38,700
No, they don't.

459
00:30:38,700 --> 00:30:43,720
They just vaguely claim it broke at exactly the points where these errors are challenged.

460
00:30:44,620 --> 00:30:49,700
But if they actually hit Layer 5 derived contradiction, the natural first suspect isn't that Lean

461
00:30:49,700 --> 00:30:50,260
is corrupt.

462
00:30:50,880 --> 00:30:55,780
The most logical conclusion is that the axioms they tried to add, like a physics equation

463
00:30:55,780 --> 00:30:58,640
that divides by zero, were internally inconsistent.

464
00:30:59,040 --> 00:31:00,880
Okay, let me play devil's advocate for a second.

465
00:31:01,260 --> 00:31:06,240
If Tone wanted to build a totally different logical universe, one that doesn't use Lean's

466
00:31:06,240 --> 00:31:08,540
native rules, how should they have done it?

467
00:31:08,540 --> 00:31:10,840
Is there a way to do that without crashing the system?

468
00:31:11,480 --> 00:31:11,720
Yes.

469
00:31:12,220 --> 00:31:14,440
This is a vital concept in computer science.

470
00:31:15,000 --> 00:31:17,260
They should have used a deep embedding.

471
00:31:17,600 --> 00:31:18,920
Deep versus shallow embedding.

472
00:31:19,020 --> 00:31:21,020
Walk me through the mechanism of how those differ.

473
00:31:21,540 --> 00:31:23,840
A shallow embedding is what Tone likely attempted.

474
00:31:24,380 --> 00:31:28,100
That's when you use the host software's native logic as your own logic.

475
00:31:28,520 --> 00:31:32,360
You just stack your new axioms on top of Lean's existing foundation.

476
00:31:32,540 --> 00:31:34,360
But you inherit all of Lean's rules.

477
00:31:34,360 --> 00:31:34,960
Right.

478
00:31:34,960 --> 00:31:38,840
If your new rules conflict with Lean's foundation, the house collapses.

479
00:31:39,540 --> 00:31:41,920
So what is a deep embedding?

480
00:31:42,720 --> 00:31:46,460
In a deep embedding, you don't adopt the host's logic at all.

481
00:31:46,920 --> 00:31:50,900
You define your own rules entirely from scratch as inductive data.

482
00:31:51,300 --> 00:31:56,580
You literally say to the software, here is the alphabet of my new language, A, B, and C.

483
00:31:56,580 --> 00:32:00,420
And here are the only three ways you are allowed to combine these letters.

484
00:32:01,240 --> 00:32:04,600
You are building an isolated sandbox inside the software.

485
00:32:04,960 --> 00:32:05,200
Yes.

486
00:32:05,860 --> 00:32:10,800
Lean's kernel is perfectly capable of hosting a deep embedding of a system that lacks an empty set,

487
00:32:11,300 --> 00:32:15,380
lacks the axiom of choice, and operates on totally alien logic.

488
00:32:15,800 --> 00:32:21,060
So Lean acts as the physical table, and you are building a custom board game on top of the table.

489
00:32:21,300 --> 00:32:24,160
The table doesn't care what the rules of the board game are.

490
00:32:24,160 --> 00:32:31,440
Exactly. So if Tone's theory cannot be deeply embedded in Lean, it is not a commentary on Lean's capability.

491
00:32:32,100 --> 00:32:34,800
It means Tone lacks a specified syntax.

492
00:32:35,500 --> 00:32:39,540
It means Tone hasn't actually defined the alphabet and the rules of their own language.

493
00:32:39,900 --> 00:32:43,960
So their failure was a failure of their own design, not a rejection by the machine.

494
00:32:44,800 --> 00:32:48,940
But wait, let me push back on this whole concept of the machine checking things.

495
00:32:48,940 --> 00:32:57,020
If a proof assistant compiles a theorem and gives you the green checkmark, doesn't the machine grant that theorem some kind of sovereign authority?

496
00:32:57,280 --> 00:32:59,880
Doesn't it verify that it is true in reality?

497
00:33:00,580 --> 00:33:01,720
Emphatically, no.

498
00:33:02,160 --> 00:33:05,740
And this is a massive misconception, even among some programmers.

499
00:33:06,600 --> 00:33:13,120
Machine checking is a strong warrant for the formal statement that was checked, and for absolutely nothing else.

500
00:33:13,120 --> 00:33:18,500
A compiled proof licenses only the specific formal scope of the math.

501
00:33:18,740 --> 00:33:21,140
It does not license physical reality.

502
00:33:21,540 --> 00:33:25,020
Because, as Fleixia notes, the map is not the territory.

503
00:33:25,420 --> 00:33:25,940
Precisely.

504
00:33:26,360 --> 00:33:31,420
If you write a perfectly valid, compiled mathematical theorem about a variable you call J,

505
00:33:32,200 --> 00:33:38,720
that theorem says absolutely nothing about the physical trajectory of a quadrotor drone until you build a bridge.

506
00:33:39,040 --> 00:33:40,180
What does that bridge look like?

507
00:33:40,180 --> 00:33:42,940
The bridge is human scientific labor.

508
00:33:43,560 --> 00:33:47,760
You have to explicitly map your mathematical variable to physical reality.

509
00:33:48,100 --> 00:33:49,840
You have to state the physical units.

510
00:33:50,200 --> 00:33:54,220
You have to explicitly list your boundary assumptions, like friction or air resistance.

511
00:33:54,840 --> 00:33:54,940
Right.

512
00:33:55,320 --> 00:34:01,020
You have to run empirical experiments to show that your mathematical model accurately predicts physical outcomes.

513
00:34:02,260 --> 00:34:05,740
Compilation is a remarkably coarse observation.

514
00:34:06,480 --> 00:34:07,340
Did the file compile?

515
00:34:07,660 --> 00:34:07,920
Sure.

516
00:34:07,920 --> 00:34:11,380
But did it compile a profound truth about gravity?

517
00:34:11,800 --> 00:34:17,440
Or did it compile a trivial tautology, like if POP is primitive, then POP is primitive?

518
00:34:18,060 --> 00:34:19,060
The compiler doesn't care.

519
00:34:19,640 --> 00:34:20,080
Exactly.

520
00:34:20,520 --> 00:34:20,720
Okay.

521
00:34:21,060 --> 00:34:24,360
So we've torn down the false accusations against Lean.

522
00:34:24,360 --> 00:34:29,700
We've shown how the machine actually works and why Tone's physics failed.

523
00:34:29,740 --> 00:34:31,920
Now it is time for the main event.

524
00:34:32,580 --> 00:34:35,380
Because Flyxion doesn't just debunk bad logic.

525
00:34:35,500 --> 00:34:38,860
Flyxion builds a new framework to explain how all logic works.

526
00:34:39,300 --> 00:34:43,280
This is where we transition to the second document, rebuilding the foundations of logic.

527
00:34:43,280 --> 00:34:50,680
After clearing away the muddy waters of the Tone controversy, Flyxion proposes something incredibly elegant.

528
00:34:50,680 --> 00:35:00,360
The claim is that we can understand all of formal logic, all of set theory, and all of type theory through a unified base of just four operators.

529
00:35:00,740 --> 00:35:01,780
Four simple verbs.

530
00:35:01,780 --> 00:35:09,340
If you can understand these four verbs, you can understand how everything from basic arithmetic to quantum field theory is formalized.

531
00:35:09,620 --> 00:35:10,620
Let's introduce the verbs.

532
00:35:11,000 --> 00:35:11,920
Number one is POP.

533
00:35:12,300 --> 00:35:13,500
Number two is REFUSE.

534
00:35:13,900 --> 00:35:15,180
Number three is BIND.

535
00:35:15,600 --> 00:35:16,880
Number four is COLLAPSE.

536
00:35:16,880 --> 00:35:19,440
I really want to ground these in something tangible.

537
00:35:19,860 --> 00:35:23,840
I was thinking about this, and I want to try translating these into a cooking analogy.

538
00:35:24,380 --> 00:35:26,100
You tell me if this tracks with the math.

539
00:35:26,400 --> 00:35:27,320
Let's test the analogy.

540
00:35:27,740 --> 00:35:28,860
Give me the first one, POP.

541
00:35:29,200 --> 00:35:31,760
POP is introducing a distinguished item.

542
00:35:32,160 --> 00:35:37,080
In cooking, POP is going to the fridge, pulling out an ingredient, and placing it on the counter.

543
00:35:37,420 --> 00:35:39,400
You are distinguishing it from the environment.

544
00:35:39,760 --> 00:35:40,620
Here is an egg.

545
00:35:40,740 --> 00:35:41,800
Here is a bag of flour.

546
00:35:42,020 --> 00:35:42,820
That tracks perfectly.

547
00:35:43,680 --> 00:35:45,820
Mathematically, POP is the introduction rule.

548
00:35:45,820 --> 00:35:52,940
It is declaring a variable, defining a constant, or pointing to a single item within a domain so that you can begin reasoning about it.

549
00:35:53,300 --> 00:35:53,780
What's next?

550
00:35:54,320 --> 00:35:54,800
REFUSE.

551
00:35:55,300 --> 00:35:58,820
Flyxion says this is restricting a domain of admissible items.

552
00:35:59,060 --> 00:36:01,660
So in our kitchen, REFUSE is filtering.

553
00:36:02,120 --> 00:36:09,960
You look at the domain of eggs on the counter, apply a condition like must not be cracked or smell terrible, and you throw away the ones that fail the condition.

554
00:36:10,460 --> 00:36:10,780
Yes.

555
00:36:11,180 --> 00:36:12,620
REFUSE is the sieve.

556
00:36:12,620 --> 00:36:17,380
It cuts away what doesn't fit your criteria, leaving you with a refined subset.

557
00:36:18,280 --> 00:36:19,200
What about the third verb?

558
00:36:19,840 --> 00:36:20,260
BIND.

559
00:36:20,740 --> 00:36:23,300
This is relating or abstracting over items.

560
00:36:23,780 --> 00:36:27,340
So bind is mixing the eggs, flour, and sugar into a batter.

561
00:36:27,720 --> 00:36:31,520
You are taking independent ingredients and relating them into a structural whole.

562
00:36:31,520 --> 00:36:39,640
Mathematically, bind is forming pairs, creating functions, or applying a universal quantifier like for all x.

563
00:36:40,080 --> 00:36:42,460
It's tying variables together so they interact.

564
00:36:43,160 --> 00:36:44,060
And the final verb.

565
00:36:44,060 --> 00:36:45,240
Collapse.

566
00:36:45,780 --> 00:36:48,880
Flyxion defines this as evaluating or identifying.

567
00:36:48,940 --> 00:36:53,560
So collapse is taking that complex mixed batter and baking it in the oven.

568
00:36:54,060 --> 00:36:59,020
The separate ingredients undergo a chemical reaction and evaluate into a single unified identity.

569
00:36:59,380 --> 00:36:59,700
A cake.

570
00:36:59,960 --> 00:37:02,020
You no longer see the flour or the egg.

571
00:37:02,100 --> 00:37:02,900
You just see the cake.

572
00:37:02,900 --> 00:37:05,340
That is a highly effective analogy.

573
00:37:05,860 --> 00:37:17,680
Because what Flyxion demonstrates mathematically is that you can deal the entire untyped lambda calculus, which is essentially the bedrock theoretical foundation of computer science, using just bind and collapse.

574
00:37:18,060 --> 00:37:22,160
You bind variables into a function, and you collapse them by evaluating the function.

575
00:37:22,160 --> 00:37:27,600
But where it gets really profound is when we look closely at refuse, right?

576
00:37:27,880 --> 00:37:28,200
Yes.

577
00:37:28,600 --> 00:37:39,260
Because Flyxion reveals that the massive historic differences between classical logic, set theory, and type theory aren't driven by some grand metaphysical war about the nature of truth.

578
00:37:39,440 --> 00:37:43,820
The differences emerge simply from where and how they allow you to use the refuse operator.

579
00:37:44,240 --> 00:37:45,060
Walk me through that.

580
00:37:45,140 --> 00:37:47,920
How does standard ZFC set theory use refuse?

581
00:37:47,920 --> 00:37:53,820
In ZFC, refuse is implemented as the axiom of separation, which we talked about earlier.

582
00:37:54,440 --> 00:37:56,580
And crucially, it is applied from the outside.

583
00:37:57,160 --> 00:38:00,260
You must start with a preexisting set, a giant bucket.

584
00:38:00,840 --> 00:38:07,880
Then you formulate a logical rule, and you apply that rule from the outside to filter the bucket and create a smaller bucket.

585
00:38:08,140 --> 00:38:11,520
And how is that different from lean, which uses type theory?

586
00:38:12,080 --> 00:38:16,000
In a dependent type theory, refuse is internalized.

587
00:38:16,000 --> 00:38:19,680
It is a subtype formation that is baked into the calculus itself.

588
00:38:20,340 --> 00:38:23,520
You don't take a generic bucket and filter it from the outside.

589
00:38:23,980 --> 00:38:27,380
The restriction is attached to the very definition of the type.

590
00:38:28,220 --> 00:38:29,700
I think I need an analogy for that.

591
00:38:30,460 --> 00:38:31,800
Think of it like a nightclub.

592
00:38:32,680 --> 00:38:40,420
ZFC is a club where anyone can walk into the building, but there's a bouncer standing in the hallway checking IDs to see if you are allowed into the VIP room.

593
00:38:41,020 --> 00:38:43,740
The filtering happens after you exist in the space.

594
00:38:44,220 --> 00:38:44,640
Oh, okay.

595
00:38:44,640 --> 00:38:49,020
Lean is a club where the ID check is built into your DNA.

596
00:38:49,820 --> 00:38:54,240
You physically cannot materialize inside the building unless you already meet the criteria.

597
00:38:54,980 --> 00:38:59,740
The compiler ensures you can never even construct an object that violates the refuse condition.

598
00:39:00,420 --> 00:39:00,640
Wow.

599
00:39:00,640 --> 00:39:09,100
So the architecture of the restriction changes how the system behaves, and the rules governing the refuse operator dictate what kind of logical universe you are living in.

600
00:39:09,780 --> 00:39:15,400
Flyxion provides a beautiful mathematical proof of this by comparing classical refuse with topological refuse.

601
00:39:15,400 --> 00:39:18,100
This is one of the deepest insights in the paper.

602
00:39:18,520 --> 00:39:22,000
It proves why certain logical laws are not universally true.

603
00:39:22,660 --> 00:39:24,180
Let's explore this carefully.

604
00:39:24,820 --> 00:39:26,060
What is classical refuse?

605
00:39:26,460 --> 00:39:30,240
In classical Boolean logic, refuse is simple subtraction.

606
00:39:30,500 --> 00:39:31,520
It is black and white.

607
00:39:31,780 --> 00:39:38,780
If your universe is all whole numbers, and you refuse all the even numbers, you are left with exactly the odd numbers.

608
00:39:38,780 --> 00:39:40,400
Okay. Simple enough.

609
00:39:41,100 --> 00:39:43,460
Now, what happens if you refuse the refusal?

610
00:39:43,840 --> 00:39:45,000
If you double refuse?

611
00:39:45,420 --> 00:39:49,360
If I refuse the odd numbers, I get back the even numbers.

612
00:39:49,500 --> 00:39:51,160
I get back exactly what I started with.

613
00:39:51,700 --> 00:39:52,280
Exactly.

614
00:39:53,240 --> 00:39:55,540
Double refusal equals the original state.

615
00:39:55,540 --> 00:40:00,360
Because of this, classical logic enjoys the law of excluded middle.

616
00:40:00,960 --> 00:40:03,440
A thing is either A or it is not A.

617
00:40:03,960 --> 00:40:05,440
There is no middle ground.

618
00:40:06,180 --> 00:40:09,360
But in topological logic, the rules of refuse change.

619
00:40:09,740 --> 00:40:10,100
Right.

620
00:40:10,720 --> 00:40:15,800
Topological logic, or constructive logic, deals with spaces and open regions.

621
00:40:16,520 --> 00:40:21,400
Imagine your universe isn't a list of discrete numbers, but a physical map of a territory.

622
00:40:21,400 --> 00:40:27,240
And the rule of this topological universe is, any region you define must be an open set.

623
00:40:27,620 --> 00:40:31,700
It must be a fuzzy region with no hard, infinitely thin boundaries.

624
00:40:32,240 --> 00:40:33,960
Okay. Fuzzy borders only.

625
00:40:34,500 --> 00:40:40,380
In this world, refuse cannot just be simple subtraction, because subtraction might leave a hard edge.

626
00:40:40,960 --> 00:40:47,920
In topology, to refuse a region means finding the largest open fuzzy region that does not overlap with your target.

627
00:40:48,340 --> 00:40:50,460
I'm tracking with you. Let's apply it to a map.

628
00:40:50,460 --> 00:40:52,440
Imagine a map of a city.

629
00:40:52,760 --> 00:40:56,260
Let's say our starting region is everywhere except City Hall.

630
00:40:56,700 --> 00:41:04,820
If we want to refuse that region, we have to find the largest open fuzzy area that is completely separate from everywhere except City Hall.

631
00:41:05,240 --> 00:41:09,360
Well, the only thing separate from everywhere except City Hall is City Hall itself.

632
00:41:09,820 --> 00:41:10,180
Right.

633
00:41:10,180 --> 00:41:15,880
But because we need an open, fuzzy boundary, we can't take the exact brick walls of City Hall.

634
00:41:16,420 --> 00:41:24,160
We take the interior rooms of City Hall, leaving a tiny, fuzzy buffer zone right at the exterior walls so we don't accidentally touch the outside.

635
00:41:24,580 --> 00:41:26,480
We get the deep interior.

636
00:41:26,480 --> 00:41:31,160
Okay. Now, what happens if we refuse that? What is the double refusal?

637
00:41:31,160 --> 00:41:33,920
We must refuse the interior of City Hall.

638
00:41:34,340 --> 00:41:38,500
We need the largest open region that does not overlap with the interior rooms.

639
00:41:39,120 --> 00:41:40,000
What does that leave us?

640
00:41:40,420 --> 00:41:45,940
It leaves us the rest of the entire city, plus that fuzzy boundary wall we had to exclude in the first step.

641
00:41:46,460 --> 00:41:46,820
Yes.

642
00:41:47,360 --> 00:41:49,980
We get the outside city, plus the walls of City Hall.

643
00:41:50,640 --> 00:41:51,980
Our region just expanded.

644
00:41:52,860 --> 00:41:55,260
Double refusal did not get us back to where we started.

645
00:41:55,260 --> 00:41:58,180
We got back strictly more than we started with.

646
00:41:59,140 --> 00:42:00,440
That is incredible to visualize.

647
00:42:00,980 --> 00:42:08,020
Because double refusal doesn't equal the original state, it mathematically proves that the law of excluded middle fails in this universe.

648
00:42:08,220 --> 00:42:08,780
Precisely.

649
00:42:09,280 --> 00:42:16,320
In a topological space, there are items like the boundary walls that the system declines to make a binary decision about.

650
00:42:16,800 --> 00:42:21,060
They aren't in the interior, but they aren't strictly in the exterior either.

651
00:42:21,060 --> 00:42:26,140
It proves that excluded middle isn't a universal truth sent down by God.

652
00:42:26,500 --> 00:42:30,200
It is just a downstream consequence of how you designed your refuse operator.

653
00:42:30,620 --> 00:42:31,560
It's a design choice.

654
00:42:32,300 --> 00:42:39,520
And understanding these operators leads us perfectly into section six, diagonal arguments and the shared skeleton of paradoxes.

655
00:42:39,700 --> 00:42:44,980
Because if these foundational systems are all just different applications of pop, refuse, bind, and collapse,

656
00:42:45,420 --> 00:42:48,940
how do they handle the great paradoxes that famously break mathematics?

657
00:42:48,940 --> 00:42:52,420
Every major foundational crisis in the history of modern math,

658
00:42:52,980 --> 00:42:57,400
Cantor's infinities, Russell's paradox, Goethe's incompleteness theorems,

659
00:42:57,600 --> 00:43:01,820
they all share the exact same architectural skeleton based on these operators.

660
00:43:02,460 --> 00:43:03,980
It's called a diagonal argument.

661
00:43:04,820 --> 00:43:06,840
Phlyoxian defines the schema very clearly.

662
00:43:07,540 --> 00:43:09,600
A diagonal argument requires three ingredients.

663
00:43:09,980 --> 00:43:13,600
A set, a function with no fixed point, and a self-referential twist.

664
00:43:14,140 --> 00:43:15,780
Walk me through the mechanism of this twist.

665
00:43:16,160 --> 00:43:17,580
I will use a classic analogy.

666
00:43:17,580 --> 00:43:18,800
Imagine a grid.

667
00:43:19,240 --> 00:43:23,240
You list out a bunch of infinite sequences of zeros and ones.

668
00:43:23,620 --> 00:43:25,380
Row one, row two, row three.

669
00:43:25,900 --> 00:43:31,720
You want to prove that you can create a new sequence that is definitely not anywhere on this infinite list.

670
00:43:32,240 --> 00:43:34,140
How do you do that if the list is infinite?

671
00:43:34,940 --> 00:43:36,640
You use the diagonal twist.

672
00:43:37,080 --> 00:43:40,340
You build a new sequence by looking at the first digit of row one.

673
00:43:40,800 --> 00:43:43,620
If it's a zero, you make your first digit a one.

674
00:43:44,020 --> 00:43:44,560
You flip it.

675
00:43:44,560 --> 00:43:47,180
Then you look at the second digit of row two.

676
00:43:47,460 --> 00:43:49,880
If it's a one, you make your second digit a zero.

677
00:43:49,880 --> 00:43:53,740
You march diagonally down the list, flipping the digits.

678
00:43:54,840 --> 00:43:59,720
So because you flipped the first digit of row one, your new sequence cannot be row one.

679
00:43:59,880 --> 00:44:02,880
Because you flip the second digit of row two, it cannot be row two.

680
00:44:03,100 --> 00:44:06,240
It mathematically escapes the entire list.

681
00:44:06,240 --> 00:44:09,120
That is the function with no fixed point.

682
00:44:09,620 --> 00:44:15,140
The flipping combined with the self-referential twist, checking the nth digit of the nth row.

683
00:44:16,100 --> 00:44:19,000
But Flyxion points out a massive trap that people fall into.

684
00:44:19,560 --> 00:44:26,020
People constantly confuse what this schema actually proves because it produces different outcomes depending on how you set it up.

685
00:44:26,020 --> 00:44:27,940
Let's compare the instances.

686
00:44:28,540 --> 00:44:29,280
Start with Cantor.

687
00:44:29,880 --> 00:44:33,660
Gay or Cantor used this diagonal schema to prove a size fact.

688
00:44:34,300 --> 00:44:40,640
He proved that the infinity of real numbers, the decimals, is strictly larger than the infinity of counting numbers.

689
00:44:41,440 --> 00:44:42,480
He didn't break math.

690
00:44:42,920 --> 00:44:49,040
He just proved that some infinities are bigger than others by showing that a power set is always larger than a space set.

691
00:44:49,040 --> 00:44:55,340
But then Bertrand Russell comes along and he uses the exact same twisting schema to break a hypothesis.

692
00:44:56,000 --> 00:44:56,120
Right.

693
00:44:56,760 --> 00:45:00,620
Russell applied it to the idea of a universal set containing all sets.

694
00:45:01,220 --> 00:45:03,300
He used a version of the barber paradox.

695
00:45:04,220 --> 00:45:10,100
Imagine a town with a barber who shaves all men and only those men who do not shave themselves.

696
00:45:10,500 --> 00:45:12,220
So does the barber shave himself?

697
00:45:12,660 --> 00:45:15,480
If he shaves himself, then he must not shave himself.

698
00:45:15,480 --> 00:45:18,980
But if he doesn't shave himself, then he must shave himself.

699
00:45:19,680 --> 00:45:21,200
It's a derived contradiction.

700
00:45:22,020 --> 00:45:25,020
Russell ran the diagonal schema and it produced a contradiction.

701
00:45:26,060 --> 00:45:29,360
By doing so, he proved that the initial hypothesis was false.

702
00:45:30,180 --> 00:45:31,420
There is no universal set.

703
00:45:32,040 --> 00:45:35,920
You must use the refuse operator to restrict sets from containing themselves.

704
00:45:36,800 --> 00:45:41,060
And then Kurt Godel uses the schema for a third completely different outcome.

705
00:45:41,740 --> 00:45:44,440
Godel applied it to the machinery of formal proofs themselves.

706
00:45:44,440 --> 00:45:49,280
He used a self-referential twist to create a mathematical sentence that translates to,

707
00:45:49,420 --> 00:45:51,540
I am not provable in this system.

708
00:45:51,760 --> 00:45:55,200
Like the liar's paradox, this statement is false.

709
00:45:55,200 --> 00:45:58,900
Similar, but with provability instead of truth.

710
00:45:59,300 --> 00:46:02,880
If the system is consistent, it cannot prove the sentence.

711
00:46:03,480 --> 00:46:05,160
Because proving it would make it false.

712
00:46:06,300 --> 00:46:09,900
So, Godel used the schema to prove a limit on provability.

713
00:46:10,660 --> 00:46:16,300
He proved that any sufficiently complex consistent system cannot prove its own consistency.

714
00:46:17,140 --> 00:46:20,900
There will always be true statements that cannot be formally proven within the system.

715
00:46:20,900 --> 00:46:28,140
So, we have one structural schema, the diagonal twist, yielding three totally different results.

716
00:46:28,520 --> 00:46:32,880
A size fact, a refutation of a hypothesis, and a limit on provability.

717
00:46:32,880 --> 00:46:36,880
Which is why Phloxian emphasizes a crucial design rule.

718
00:46:37,400 --> 00:46:42,780
When you invoke a diagonal argument, you must state exactly what kind of conclusion you are drawing.

719
00:46:43,540 --> 00:46:47,640
You cannot use Cantor's size fact to claim a girdle-like limit on provability.

720
00:46:48,300 --> 00:46:51,360
You cannot use Russell's refutation to claim a size fact.

721
00:46:51,680 --> 00:46:55,640
They share a skeleton, but they are entirely different beasts.

722
00:46:55,860 --> 00:46:58,560
This brings us to a huge philosophical pivot.

723
00:46:58,560 --> 00:47:04,280
If ZFC, Lean, and all these foundations are just different rule sets for the four operators,

724
00:47:04,780 --> 00:47:09,280
and if they all have to navigate these paradoxes, how do we choose which one to use?

725
00:47:09,780 --> 00:47:11,980
Are we just picking our favorite flavor of logic?

726
00:47:12,560 --> 00:47:15,900
This is one of the most clarifying parts of the entire deep dive.

727
00:47:16,760 --> 00:47:22,160
Phloxian argues that a foundational system like ZFC or Lean is essentially a convention.

728
00:47:22,800 --> 00:47:24,180
It's not a divine truth.

729
00:47:24,340 --> 00:47:28,400
It's a protocol, like choosing a unit of length or a base for counting.

730
00:47:28,560 --> 00:47:31,660
Right, we count in base 10, the digits 0 through 9.

731
00:47:32,680 --> 00:47:36,460
Computers count in base 2, binary, which is just 0 and 1.

732
00:47:36,980 --> 00:47:39,740
But to prove how arbitrary conventions can be,

733
00:47:40,360 --> 00:47:43,340
Phloxian introduces a wild, brilliant example.

734
00:47:44,180 --> 00:47:50,020
The Uni-DIMRI system, base 1.5, a non-integer radix of 3 over 2.

735
00:47:50,020 --> 00:47:54,560
I had to read this section three times, but it is so cool once you visualize it.

736
00:47:55,120 --> 00:48:01,040
Instead of carrying over a digit when you hit 10, the rule is based on exploding dots.

737
00:48:01,500 --> 00:48:04,120
Imagine you have a row of empty buckets on the floor.

738
00:48:04,820 --> 00:48:09,660
Bucket 0 is on the right, bucket 1 to its left, then bucket 2, and so on.

739
00:48:09,660 --> 00:48:17,560
The rule for base 1.5 is, if you put three dots in one bucket, they immediately explode,

740
00:48:18,000 --> 00:48:22,840
disappear from that bucket, and magically reappear as two dots in the bucket to the left.

741
00:48:23,180 --> 00:48:28,280
Three dots in bucket k explode to become two dots in bucket k plus 1.

742
00:48:28,280 --> 00:48:32,700
And Phloxian shows this perfectly respects the math of base 1.5,

743
00:48:32,860 --> 00:48:39,100
because 3 times 3 halves to the power of k exactly equals 2 times 3 halves to the power of k plus 1.

744
00:48:39,480 --> 00:48:42,440
Let's walk through it slowly so the listener can visualize it.

745
00:48:43,080 --> 00:48:46,960
How do you write the standard number 10 in base 1.5?

746
00:48:47,060 --> 00:48:48,500
Okay, I actually did this on paper.

747
00:48:48,940 --> 00:48:52,660
You start by dropping 10 dots into the first bucket on the right, bucket 0.

748
00:48:53,040 --> 00:48:54,920
And what happened during bucket 0?

749
00:48:55,060 --> 00:48:58,240
The rule is every group of three dots explodes.

750
00:48:58,580 --> 00:49:01,100
Since I have 10 dots, I have three groups of three.

751
00:49:01,420 --> 00:49:03,060
So nine dots explode.

752
00:49:03,360 --> 00:49:05,060
They leave one dot behind in bucket 0,

753
00:49:05,260 --> 00:49:11,060
and those three explosions send six dots, three groups of two, over to bucket 1 on the left.

754
00:49:11,680 --> 00:49:11,920
Perfect.

755
00:49:12,320 --> 00:49:15,900
So bucket 0 has one dot, bucket 1 has six dots.

756
00:49:16,540 --> 00:49:17,820
What happens in bucket 1?

757
00:49:18,260 --> 00:49:21,660
Bucket 1 has six dots, which is exactly two groups of three.

758
00:49:22,080 --> 00:49:23,280
Both groups explode.

759
00:49:23,280 --> 00:49:26,540
They leave exactly zero dots behind in bucket 1,

760
00:49:26,540 --> 00:49:31,500
and they send four dots, two groups of two, over to bucket 2 on the left.

761
00:49:31,500 --> 00:49:38,220
So reading right to left, we have one dot, then zero dots, and now four dots in bucket 2.

762
00:49:38,600 --> 00:49:40,180
What happens to the four dots?

763
00:49:40,700 --> 00:49:42,300
Bucket 2 has four dots.

764
00:49:42,600 --> 00:49:44,080
One group of three explodes.

765
00:49:44,500 --> 00:49:48,640
It leaves one dot behind in bucket 2 and sends two dots over to bucket 3.

766
00:49:48,640 --> 00:49:53,160
And bucket 3 has two dots, which isn't enough to trigger an explosion,

767
00:49:53,560 --> 00:49:55,400
so the chain reaction stops.

768
00:49:55,780 --> 00:49:56,080
Right.

769
00:49:56,280 --> 00:50:02,620
So reading the buckets from left to right, we have two dots, one dot, zero dots, and one dot.

770
00:50:03,080 --> 00:50:10,320
Therefore, the number 10, written in base 1.5, is the string 2, 1, 0, 1.

771
00:50:10,320 --> 00:50:18,560
And if you run the polynomial expansion, 2 times 27 over 8 plus 1 times 9 over 4 plus 0 plus 1,

772
00:50:18,820 --> 00:50:20,260
it equals exactly 10.

773
00:50:21,040 --> 00:50:21,840
It works flawlessly.

774
00:50:22,400 --> 00:50:29,580
Every single arithmetic sentence, every addition, every multiplication remains mathematically true in base 1.5.

775
00:50:30,000 --> 00:50:31,620
The math does not change.

776
00:50:32,100 --> 00:50:34,100
The math is perfectly valid.

777
00:50:34,720 --> 00:50:37,780
So why on earth don't we use base 1.5?

778
00:50:37,780 --> 00:50:39,840
Why don't we balance our checkbooks in it?

779
00:50:40,320 --> 00:50:42,080
Because of the computational cost.

780
00:50:42,900 --> 00:50:45,360
Flightsheen calculates the exact burden of this convention.

781
00:50:46,100 --> 00:50:48,920
First, it takes way more digits to write a number.

782
00:50:49,700 --> 00:50:55,680
The length of a number string in base 1.5 is asymptotically 1.7 times longer than binary,

783
00:50:56,100 --> 00:50:58,380
and 5.6 times longer than base 10.

784
00:50:58,960 --> 00:51:02,620
To write a number like 1 million, you would need 32 digits.

785
00:51:03,140 --> 00:51:05,640
That would make reading a phone number impossible.

786
00:51:06,180 --> 00:51:08,260
And the second cost is redundancy.

787
00:51:08,260 --> 00:51:15,980
In base 10, every random string of digits you can think of, like 4, 7, 2, 9, is a valid whole number.

788
00:51:16,760 --> 00:51:20,640
In base 1.5, you only use the digits 0, 1, and 2.

789
00:51:21,120 --> 00:51:24,340
But most random strings of those digits don't even evaluate to integers.

790
00:51:24,600 --> 00:51:26,600
They evaluate to weird, messy fractions.

791
00:51:26,600 --> 00:51:34,100
Out of 59,000 possible 10-digit strings, only 186 of them actually evaluate to whole integers.

792
00:51:34,860 --> 00:51:39,200
So the system is wildly inefficient and bloated with junk data.

793
00:51:39,200 --> 00:51:44,520
Yes. And this is the profound point Flyxion is making about our mathematical foundations.

794
00:51:45,320 --> 00:51:47,680
Does the physical universe mandate base 10?

795
00:51:48,140 --> 00:51:48,400
No.

796
00:51:49,060 --> 00:51:52,600
Does metaphysical reality demand ZFC or lean?

797
00:51:52,980 --> 00:51:53,220
No.

798
00:51:53,900 --> 00:51:58,940
We choose foundational bases not because they are the truest reflection of God's universe,

799
00:51:59,180 --> 00:52:02,660
but because they provide the best reproducibility protocol.

800
00:52:02,660 --> 00:52:05,520
That phrase, reproducibility protocol.

801
00:52:06,100 --> 00:52:11,320
We choose English over Morse code for a deep dive because it transmits data faster and more reliably.

802
00:52:11,580 --> 00:52:12,120
Precisely.

803
00:52:12,520 --> 00:52:18,340
We choose systems like ZFC because they offer low cognitive cost, high adoption among humans,

804
00:52:18,500 --> 00:52:21,380
and they allow us to share and verify results easily.

805
00:52:21,940 --> 00:52:26,640
Imagine if half the mathematicians in the world used ZFC with the axiom of choice,

806
00:52:26,640 --> 00:52:32,320
and the other half used a completely different framework, but nobody told each other which one they were using.

807
00:52:32,860 --> 00:52:33,580
It would be chaos.

808
00:52:34,000 --> 00:52:38,420
They would produce correct, brilliant theorems that were mutually unusable.

809
00:52:38,960 --> 00:52:40,500
They couldn't chain their proofs together.

810
00:52:40,960 --> 00:52:46,160
So ZFC, type theory, base 10, they are just agreed upon conventions.

811
00:52:46,840 --> 00:52:50,580
They are the network protocols, like TCPIP for the internet,

812
00:52:50,760 --> 00:52:53,680
that allow humanity to build a shared tower of knowledge.

813
00:52:53,680 --> 00:52:58,620
Exactly. They give us comparability, a common language to verify our truths.

814
00:52:59,180 --> 00:53:04,440
This takes us to the final, most speculative, and frankly, most breathtaking part of the papers.

815
00:53:04,740 --> 00:53:08,280
We've talked about math, software, and human conventions.

816
00:53:08,720 --> 00:53:12,880
Now we are returning to the physical universe one last time.

817
00:53:13,080 --> 00:53:17,560
Let's go back to the four operators, pop, refuse, bind, and collapse.

818
00:53:18,000 --> 00:53:22,740
We talked about collapses baking the cake, evaluating an equation,

819
00:53:22,740 --> 00:53:24,940
quotienting items together.

820
00:53:25,900 --> 00:53:31,160
When you apply the collapse operator, what actually happens to the information in the system?

821
00:53:31,660 --> 00:53:32,920
Well, it gets lost.

822
00:53:33,500 --> 00:53:39,400
Once the cake is baked, you can't reverse engineer it to tell me exactly which farm the egg came from.

823
00:53:39,820 --> 00:53:41,580
The independent data is gone.

824
00:53:42,060 --> 00:53:45,500
Mathematically, this loss of information is known as Shannon entropy.

825
00:53:45,500 --> 00:53:50,800
In information theory, entropy is a measure of unpredictability, or lost data.

826
00:53:51,600 --> 00:54:00,560
Flexium proves mathematically that the lost entropy generated by a collapse operator is precisely equal to the conditional Shannon entropy.

827
00:54:00,980 --> 00:54:02,540
Let me make sure I understand that.

828
00:54:02,540 --> 00:54:08,400
If you group items together, if you coarse-grain the data, you lose information.

829
00:54:08,960 --> 00:54:09,160
Yes.

830
00:54:09,800 --> 00:54:17,700
The coarser the collapse, the more unique items you mash together into a single identity, the more information is discarded from the system.

831
00:54:17,700 --> 00:54:25,460
And Flyxion connects this mathematical loss of information to a theoretical physics framework called the RSVP plenum.

832
00:54:25,680 --> 00:54:27,760
What is the RSVP plenum?

833
00:54:28,200 --> 00:54:33,200
It is a highly speculative advanced framework that attempts to explain gravity,

834
00:54:33,700 --> 00:54:38,260
but not as a fundamental independent force of nature like electromagnetism.

835
00:54:38,520 --> 00:54:43,480
The RSVP plenum tries to explain gravity as an entropic descent.

836
00:54:43,480 --> 00:54:47,420
Wait, how does gravity connect to the collapse operator?

837
00:54:48,780 --> 00:54:51,300
Flyxion outlines a heuristic conjecture.

838
00:54:52,280 --> 00:55:01,660
What if the entropy field we observe in physics is literally just the accumulated lost entropy from a series of macroscopic collapses of microscopic data?

839
00:55:02,120 --> 00:55:02,520
Whoa.

840
00:55:03,240 --> 00:55:04,080
Think about it.

841
00:55:04,300 --> 00:55:09,360
When you zoom out and look at a planet, you aren't tracking the position of every single atom.

842
00:55:09,840 --> 00:55:12,380
You are applying a massive collapse operator.

843
00:55:12,380 --> 00:55:18,200
You are coarse-graining a trillion, trillion particles into one identity called a planet.

844
00:55:19,120 --> 00:55:22,680
And by doing that, I am discarding an astronomical amount of information.

845
00:55:23,340 --> 00:55:23,580
Yes.

846
00:55:24,740 --> 00:55:26,300
And Flyxion's conjecture asks,

847
00:55:26,940 --> 00:55:30,300
What if gravity is just the universe coarse-graining itself?

848
00:55:30,960 --> 00:55:34,680
What if gravity doesn't require an extra primitive force in our ontology?

849
00:55:34,680 --> 00:55:41,900
It would just be the natural mathematical cost, the entropic shadow, of zooming out and identifying particles together.

850
00:55:42,660 --> 00:55:45,900
As the universe evaluates itself, information is lost.

851
00:55:46,400 --> 00:55:50,660
And that loss physically manifests as entropic gravity pulling mass together.

852
00:55:50,920 --> 00:55:54,780
That is one of the most beautiful analogies for gravity I have ever heard.

853
00:55:54,900 --> 00:55:56,660
But I have to ask the critical question.

854
00:55:56,660 --> 00:55:58,060
Is this proven?

855
00:55:58,560 --> 00:56:01,640
Did Flyxion just mathematically prove what gravity is?

856
00:56:02,200 --> 00:56:03,100
Emphatically, no.

857
00:56:03,380 --> 00:56:04,140
And this is vital.

858
00:56:04,820 --> 00:56:07,300
Flyxion is exceptionally careful to state this.

859
00:56:07,820 --> 00:56:10,580
It is strictly a heuristic analogy right now.

860
00:56:10,800 --> 00:56:12,620
It is a mathematical hypothesis.

861
00:56:13,200 --> 00:56:15,320
So what would it actually take to prove it?

862
00:56:15,320 --> 00:56:20,320
Flyxion lays out exactly the grueling physics labor required to prove it.

863
00:56:21,060 --> 00:56:24,040
One would have to state a concrete lattice model of the plenum.

864
00:56:24,560 --> 00:56:27,740
Then compute the thermodynamic limit as the lattice scales up.

865
00:56:28,320 --> 00:56:31,300
Then calculate the specific entropic forces.

866
00:56:31,840 --> 00:56:38,040
And finally, match those results precisely to Poisson-type gravity equations that govern our observed reality.

867
00:56:38,760 --> 00:56:43,520
A negative result at any single one of those steps would instantly falsify the conjecture.

868
00:56:43,520 --> 00:56:46,360
It remains a massive open problem in physics.

869
00:56:46,840 --> 00:56:50,920
That distinction is so important because it highlights exactly what Tone did wrong.

870
00:56:51,480 --> 00:56:57,380
Flyxion isn't taking a cool mathematical analogy and declaring it as undisputed ontological law.

871
00:56:57,840 --> 00:56:58,400
Exactly.

872
00:56:59,320 --> 00:57:06,960
Flyxion respects the difference between an elegant mathematical model, a checked software compilation, and empirical testable physics.

873
00:57:06,960 --> 00:57:16,460
That respect for scope, knowing exactly what your map describes, and acknowledging that it is not the territory, is the ultimate lesson of these papers.

874
00:57:17,160 --> 00:57:17,880
What a journey.

875
00:57:18,520 --> 00:57:23,860
We started with a rebellion, claiming that mathematical software is a corrupt metaphysical overlord.

876
00:57:24,340 --> 00:57:27,340
And we've seen how that is a fundamental category error.

877
00:57:27,880 --> 00:57:30,900
Formal systems like Lean are not philosophical dictators.

878
00:57:30,900 --> 00:57:35,260
They are highly specific, auditable tools that track our logic.

879
00:57:36,080 --> 00:57:44,000
We learned that the foundations of math, whether ZFC or dependent type theory, rely on shared conventions to make knowledge reproducible.

880
00:57:44,700 --> 00:57:46,500
They are the base 10 of our logic.

881
00:57:46,500 --> 00:57:53,400
We saw how the simple verbs pop, refuse, bind, and collapse are the underlying heartbeat of everything.

882
00:57:53,820 --> 00:58:02,520
From proving 2 plus 2 equals 4, to defining the fuzzy borders of topological spaces, to theorizing about the entropic nature of gravity itself.

883
00:58:03,340 --> 00:58:11,320
It all comes down to where you place your restrictions, how you filter your domain, and who checks the ID at the door of your mathematical club.

884
00:58:11,320 --> 00:58:16,320
But before we sign off, I want to leave you, the listener, with a provocative thought to mull over.

885
00:58:16,720 --> 00:58:21,140
We've talked extensively today about how humans pick foundational conventions based on our own limitations.

886
00:58:21,460 --> 00:58:24,160
We choose base 10 because it's easy for our brains.

887
00:58:24,300 --> 00:58:28,200
We use ZFC because it's a great shared language with low cognitive cost.

888
00:58:28,340 --> 00:58:33,200
But what happens as we move into an era of advanced, self-improving AI?

889
00:58:33,460 --> 00:58:35,680
Oh, this is a fascinating extrapolation.

890
00:58:35,680 --> 00:58:44,960
If an artificial intelligence is tasked with generating millions of complex mathematical proofs or discovering new physics, it will not care about human cognitive cost.

891
00:58:45,420 --> 00:58:46,180
It doesn't get tired.

892
00:58:46,620 --> 00:58:50,060
It might look at our logic and decide to optimize its own internal foundation.

893
00:58:50,480 --> 00:58:56,880
It might invent a completely alien, refused discipline that operates in 100-dimensional topological space.

894
00:58:56,880 --> 00:59:07,940
It might decide that a bizarre, non-integer RADX system, far more complex and bloated than base 1.5, is actually slightly more computationally efficient for its specific silicon hardware.

895
00:59:08,420 --> 00:59:13,700
The machine could build a deep embedding of a mathematical language that we simply cannot conceptualize.

896
00:59:14,220 --> 00:59:14,660
Exactly.

897
00:59:15,140 --> 00:59:18,400
We might end up with a universe of profound mathematical truths.

898
00:59:18,880 --> 00:59:23,500
The AI might solve quantum gravity or flawlessly prove the cure to a genetic disease.

899
00:59:23,500 --> 00:59:27,020
And those proofs will be perfectly verified by the machine's kernel.

900
00:59:27,640 --> 00:59:32,860
But the foundational language of those proofs will be entirely incomprehensible to the human mind.

901
00:59:33,520 --> 00:59:40,880
The machine will hand us the cure, and when we ask to see the proof, it will hand us a trillion lines of exploding dot alien logic.

902
00:59:41,700 --> 00:59:47,980
Are we prepared for a future where the map is perfectly, rigorously accurate, but humans can no longer read the legend?

903
00:59:47,980 --> 00:59:52,960
That is the ultimate humbling consequence of the map not being the territory.

904
00:59:53,500 --> 00:59:55,860
The map maker might outgrow us.

905
00:59:56,440 --> 01:00:00,680
Something to think about the next time you trust a calculator or hit compile on a piece of code.

906
01:00:01,360 --> 01:00:02,740
Keep questioning your assumptions.

907
01:00:03,300 --> 01:00:04,460
Check your own logical footprints.

908
01:00:04,680 --> 01:00:08,940
And always remember, an empty cardboard box is still a box.

909
01:00:09,480 --> 01:00:10,620
Thanks for diving deep with us.

910
01:00:10,620 --> 01:00:11,600
You're listening to science.

911
